### What is type erasure?

At compile time the compiler fully checks generics. But after checking, it erases the type parameter: an unbounded `T` becomes `Object`, a bounded `T extends Number` becomes `Number`, and it inserts casts at call sites. The consequence is that `List<String>` and `List<Integer>` are the same class at runtime.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class ErasureDemo {

    public void demonstrateErasure() {
        List<String> accounts = new ArrayList<>();
        List<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();

        // At runtime, both are just ArrayList
        System.out.println(
            accounts.getClass() == numbers.getClass()); // true

        // The generic type is gone at runtime
        System.out.println(
            accounts.getClass().getName()); // java.util.ArrayList
    }
}
```

### Why can you not write new T()?

At runtime `T` is erased to its bound, so the JVM does not know which constructor to call. The standard workaround is to pass a `Class<T>` token and use reflection.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class GenericRepository<T> {

    private final Class<T> entityClass;

    // Pass Class<T> token to recover type at runtime
    public GenericRepository(Class<T> entityClass) {
        this.entityClass = entityClass;
    }

    public T createNew() throws Exception {
        // new T() is impossible, so use reflection
        return entityClass.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();
    }
}
```

```java
// Usage
var repo = new GenericRepository<>(SavingsAccount.class);
SavingsAccount account = repo.createNew();
```

### Why can you not write instanceof List<String>?

The runtime only has raw `List`, with no memory of the element type. You can only test `instanceof List<?>` (unbounded wildcard).

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;

public class InstanceofDemo {

    public void checkType(Object obj) {
        // COMPILE ERROR: cannot check parameterized type
        // if (obj instanceof List<String>) { }

        // OK: unbounded wildcard
        if (obj instanceof List<?> list) {
            System.out.println("It is a List of size " + list.size());
        }
    }
}
```

### Why can you not create generic arrays?

Arrays are reified: they carry their component type and check it at runtime with store checks. This directly conflicts with erased generics.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class GenericArrayDemo {

    public void arrayProblem() {
        // COMPILE ERROR: cannot create generic array
        // List<String>[] accountLists = new List<String>[10];

        // Workaround: use List of Lists
        List<List<String>> accountLists = new ArrayList<>();

        // Or use Object[] with unchecked cast
        @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
        List<String>[] arr = (List<String>[]) new List<?>[10];
    }
}
```

### Why can you not overload by generic type?

After erasure, methods that differ only by generic type argument have the same raw signature and clash.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class OverloadClashDemo {

    // COMPILE ERROR: both erase to process(List)
    // public void process(List<String> accounts) { }
    // public void process(List<BigDecimal> amounts) { }

    // Fix: use different method names
    public void processAccounts(List<String> accounts) {
        System.out.println("Processing accounts");
    }

    public void processAmounts(List<BigDecimal> amounts) {
        System.out.println("Processing amounts");
    }
}
```

### What is the PECS rule?

PECS stands for Producer Extends, Consumer Super. Use `? extends T` when reading from a structure (producer) and `? super T` when writing into it (consumer).

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class PecsDemo {

    // PRODUCER: read accounts out (? extends Account)
    public BigDecimal sumBalances(
            List<? extends Account> accounts) {
        BigDecimal total = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        for (Account acc : accounts) {
            total = total.add(acc.getBalance());
        }
        return total;
        // Can read as Account
        // Cannot add to list (except null)
    }

    // CONSUMER: write accounts in (? super SavingsAccount)
    public void onboardNewAccounts(
            List<? super SavingsAccount> target) {
        target.add(new SavingsAccount("SAV2001",
                new BigDecimal("50000.00")));
        target.add(new SavingsAccount("SAV2002",
                new BigDecimal("75000.00")));
        // Can add SavingsAccount
        // When reading, only get Object back
    }
}
```

### How does Collections.copy demonstrate PECS?

The canonical PECS example reads from an extends source and writes to a super destination.

```java
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

// Signature: copy(List<? super T> dest, List<? extends T> src)
// src is PRODUCER (extends): we read from it
// dest is CONSUMER (super): we write into it

List<SavingsAccount> savings = List.of(
    new SavingsAccount("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("50000.00")));

List<Account> allAccounts = new ArrayList<>(savings);

// SavingsAccount extends Account:
// src: List<? extends Account> ← List<SavingsAccount> OK
// dest: List<? super Account> could accept List<Object>
Collections.copy(allAccounts, savings); // Compiles
```

### How is ? extends different from ? super? (Comparison)

| Feature | `? extends T` (Producer) | `? super T` (Consumer) |
|---|---|---|
| Reading | Safe: every element is at least T | Unsafe: only get Object back |
| Writing | Cannot add (except null) | Safe: can add T values |
| Use when | Structure is a source you read from | Structure is a sink you write into |
| Example | `sumBalances(List<? extends Account>)` | `onboard(List<? super SavingsAccount>)` |
| Mnemonic | PE: Producer Extends | CS: Consumer Super |

### What are common mistakes with erasure and PECS?

Getting the wildcard wrong is one of the most common sources of confusing "cannot be applied" compile errors.

```java
// WRONG: using extends when writing
public void addAccount(List<? extends Account> list) {
    // list.add(new SavingsAccount(...)); // COMPILE ERROR!
    // Cannot add to a producer
}

// CORRECT: use super when writing
public void addAccount(List<? super SavingsAccount> list) {
    list.add(new SavingsAccount("SAV2001",
            new BigDecimal("50000.00"))); // OK
}

// WRONG: thinking generics exist at runtime
if (list instanceof List<String>) { } // COMPILE ERROR

// CORRECT: use unbounded wildcard
if (list instanceof List<?>) { } // OK
```

### What about unchecked cast warnings?

Erasure is why you see `@SuppressWarnings("unchecked")` on casts like `(T) obj`. The runtime cannot verify the cast, so the compiler warns you.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class UncheckedCastDemo {

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public <T> T safeCast(Object obj, Class<T> type) {
        if (type.isInstance(obj)) {
            return (T) obj; // Unchecked, but safe with isInstance guard
        }
        throw new ClassCastException(
            "Cannot cast to " + type.getName());
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Erasure is why generics are a compile-time feature with zero runtime cost. It is also why you sometimes pass a Class token or a TypeReference to recover type information at runtime, as Jackson and Spring do. PECS is not academic: getting the wildcard wrong is a very common cause of "cannot be applied" compile errors in real APIs. If you both read and write, use an exact `T` with no wildcard.
